<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Laceration of the left index finger (S61.211A)</content>. Simple dorsal laceration without tendon, nerve, or nail injury. The chisel slipped because of weak pinch grip from thumb-base osteoarthritis while he worked without his splint. [S]<list><item>Irrigation and repair with four 5-0 nylon sutures under a digital block.</item><item>Tdap today.</item><item>Keep the dressing dry for 48 hours; no carving with the left hand until the sutures are out.</item><item>Suture removal by the nurse in 10 to 12 days.</item><item>Return the same day for redness, swelling, pus, fever, or loss of finger movement.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Left hand osteoarthritis (M19.042)</content>. The injury shows how grip weakness makes tool work less safe. He has had two of his four hand therapy sessions. [S]<list><item>Wear the thumb splint whenever he carves.</item><item>Ask the hand therapist at the next session about padded chisel grips and a wrist-strap mallet.</item><item>Acetaminophen for wound pain; no oral NSAIDs.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Tdap vaccine for wound management, last dose more than 10 years ago [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse visit for suture removal in 10 to 12 days. Hand osteoarthritis review in July as planned. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-13: hand osteoarthritis review three months after a thumb-base injection. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
